METHOD
  Built the worktree via the lead's serve.sh (preupload variant, port 8977,
  frozen real data + 7 browser-named exports: 3 identical/disabled,
  4 detectable). Ran it as a background process (`run_in_background`) so it
  survived across tool calls — first attempt used a bare `nohup ... &`
  inside a plain Bash call, which got reaped when that call returned; this
  produced two rounds of misleading axe output (documented below and
  discarded) until I switched to `run_in_background` and reconfirmed the
  server was actually serving (curl 200 on /static/css/tailwind.css and
  /filemanager) before composing pages.
  Fetched real server-rendered fragments:
    GET /filemanager                              -> shell.html (page shell)
    GET /explorer/import/scan  (HX-Request:true)   -> scan.html (7 entries:
       bk_download(1).csv, bk_download(2).csv, bk_download.csv = identical/
       disabled with "(identical to <name>, will be skipped)"; bk_download(3)
       /(4)/(5).csv, creditCard24-25.csv = checked with a detected
       usaa-credit-card/usaa-checking selection)
    POST /explorer/import (4 detected files, delete_source=true)
                                                    -> result.html (4/4
       "— imported", e.g. "imported as usaa-credit-card_2026-07-01_to_
       2026-08-27.csv (USAA Credit Card), source file deleted")
  Composed shell.html + scan.html into #import-scan-list and shell.html +
  result.html into #import-result (regex innerHTML splice, matching what
  htmx produces in the browser). Neutralized the page's own theme
  auto-detect boot script (localStorage/prefers-color-scheme -> adds
  "dark" class) in the composed copy so the test harness's forced
  `class="light"` / `class="dark"` on <html> actually controls the theme
  instead of being overridden at parse time — the headless Chrome instance
  axe-core CLI drives apparently defaults prefers-color-scheme:dark, which
  silently forced BOTH themes to render dark in my first pass (both
  axe-light.json and axe-dark.json showed identical dark-theme computed
  colors for the label/select — a harness bug I caught by diffing the two
  JSON files against each other, not a page bug). Added `<base
  href="http://127.0.0.1:8977/">` so the composed file:// page's
  absolute-path /static/css/tailwind.css resolves against the live
  instance's real built stylesheet.

