DISCREPANCY NOTE (for the record, not relied on for the verdict):
  Mid-session the throwaway server died between tool calls (a plain
  `nohup ... &` inside a foreground Bash call was reaped when that call
  returned) and I didn't notice immediately; the next axe pass against
  stale/partially-unreachable assets produced two inconsistent results —
  one where light and dark showed identical (wrongly dark) computed
  colours, and one showing a spurious 5-occurrence "target-size" violation
  (nav logo, #theme-toggle-mobile, #mobile-nav-toggle, "Load Test Data"
  link, "Clear All" button). I caught this by cross-checking axe's own
  fgColor/bgColor between the two theme runs (they were identical, which is
  impossible for a correctly-forced light vs dark render) and by re-curling
  the CSS URL (000/timeout). I restarted the server with `run_in_background`
  (survives across tool calls), re-fetched shell/scan/result fresh, verified
  200s on /static/css/tailwind.css and /filemanager first, and re-ran
  everything — the corroborated result (0 violations, contrast numbers that
  independently match two different measurement methods: axe's own
  color-contrast `any.data` and a getComputedStyle probe script dumped via
  `google-chrome --headless=new --dump-dom`) is what this verdict is based
  on. For completeness: even if the spurious 5 target-size hits had been
  real, all 5 elements are confirmed byte-identical to master (`git diff
  master -- web/templates/layouts/base.html` empty; filemanager.html's
  "Load Test Data"/"Clear All" buttons at lines ~205-224, outside the
  diff's only hunk at line 576) — PRE-EXISTING, would be an OBSERVATION,
  not a FAIL for IM2 either way.

OBSERVATIONS (non-blocking):
  1. Axe "incomplete" color-contrast on the nav logo (`.text-xl` over a
     gradient nav) — PRE-EXISTING (web/templates/layouts/base.html, 0-line
     diff vs master). Axe cannot resolve a gradient background
     automatically; out of scope for IM2.
  2. The "(could not detect the account — choose one)" note is a DOM
     sibling of its `<select>`, not bound via `aria-describedby`. It
     satisfies A-9 (it's text, not colour-only) and sits immediately after
     the select in reading order, so it's discoverable in browse/virtual-
     cursor mode, but a screen-reader user who tabs directly select-to-
     select may not hear it announced on focus. The constitution doesn't
     require programmatic binding, so this isn't a FAIL — worth a backlog
     line if the team wants to strengthen it (`aria-describedby` pointing
     at the note's id).
  3. This real fixture's four "detectable" exports were all successfully
     detected, so the "(could not detect...)" string was never actually
     rendered by the live server in this check — verified instead by
     class-identity with the "(identical to ...)" string, which did render
     and was measured directly (see A-3). The ambiguous-detection code path
     itself is exercised by internal/handlers/explorer/im2_test.go /
     handlers_test.go (not this checker's lane).

